-- lib/meaning.cub — the degenerate case of meaning computed from the record (docs/language-derived-
-- from-the-record.cub, §§2–4): the case where every identification is equality of characters and every
-- path is refl. The record is every text the source holds (each module as the file it is) and every turn
-- said since; an occurrence is an event of the record. The present context is the turn; a past
-- occurrence is selected when its context equals the present one character for character, and the most
-- context selected is the longest end of the turn the record holds. What followed each selected
-- occurrence, to the end of its line, is shown as it is: with every path refl, transport is the
-- identity, and the family is printed once where it agrees and branch by branch where it parts.
-- What the paper's construction has and this does not (Avik, 2026-11-18: "correct ai is not achieved
-- simply by reducing the problem substring search"): identifications that are not equality of
-- characters (§2: identification is the participant's act on its record, the paths of Occ_i(w)); a
-- context identified through the identifications of the occurrences it is made of, one level up and
-- higher (§3: the record with its identifications is a higher type, and selection is a fiber of it);
-- transport of what followed along the path that identified its context with the present one (§4), not
-- a copy; and determinacy as contractibility of the transported family (§4), not agreement of a prefix.
-- This file is the character-level fiber over refl paths, a lower bound the construction refines.
-- Memory is local (memory-is-local): no process holds the whole record. It is read one text at a time,
-- each held as its bytes, and the search reads the bytes where they are in compiled code (#jit). The longest end of
-- the turn a text holds is the longest prefix of the reversed turn the reversed text holds, which the
-- Knuth–Morris–Pratt automaton of the reversed turn finds in one pass: Θ(ℓ + m) for a text of ℓ bytes and
-- a turn of m, so a query is Θ(n + T·m) over the n bytes of the T texts, holding one text at a time. The
-- end considered is the turn's last 1024 bytes.
-- Used by `hyperactive chat` (lib/chat.cub) and by the server's /chat and /api/meaning, which hold it in
-- their running process.
